# React Component Refactoring Best Practices

This guide outlines best practices for refactoring React components in our codebase.

## When to Refactor

### Signs a Component Needs Refactoring

- **Excessive Length**: Component exceeds 300 lines of code
- **Too Many Props**: Component has more than 8-10 props
- **Multiple Responsibilities**: Component handles multiple unrelated concerns
- **Complex Rendering Logic**: Nested conditionals make the JSX hard to follow
- **Performance Issues**: Component re-renders too frequently or handles expensive operations
- **Duplicate Code**: Similar logic appears in multiple components
- **Deeply Nested Components**: Component renders multiple levels of nested children
- **Tightly Coupled Logic**: Business logic is intermixed with UI rendering code

## Refactoring Strategies

### Component Decomposition

- Extract logical sections into smaller, focused components
- Create reusable UI components for repeating patterns
- Separate complex stateful logic from presentational components

```tsx
// ❌ Bad: Monolithic component
function UserDashboard({ user, data, permissions, ...otherProps }) {
  // 200+ lines of code with multiple responsibilities
}

// ✅ Good: Decomposed components
function UserDashboard({ user, data, permissions }) {
  return (
    <DashboardLayout>
      <UserHeader user={user} />
      <PermissionsGuard permissions={permissions}>
        <DataVisualizer data={data} />
      </PermissionsGuard>
      <UserActions user={user} />
    </DashboardLayout>
  );
}
```

### Extract Custom Hooks

- Move stateful logic and side effects into custom hooks
- Create hooks for reusable behaviors (form handling, API calls, animations)
- Keep hooks focused on a single responsibility

```tsx
// ❌ Bad: Logic embedded in component
function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    async function fetchUser() {
      try {
        setLoading(true);
        const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
        const data = await response.json();
        setUser(data);
      } catch (err) {
        setError(err);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    }
    
    fetchUser();
  }, [userId]);
  
  // Rendering logic...
}

// ✅ Good: Logic extracted to custom hook
function useUser(userId) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    async function fetchUser() {
      try {
        setLoading(true);
        const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
        const data = await response.json();
        setUser(data);
      } catch (err) {
        setError(err);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    }
    
    fetchUser();
  }, [userId]);

  return { user, loading, error };
}

function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  const { user, loading, error } = useUser(userId);
  
  // Simplified rendering logic...
}
```

### Simplify Conditional Rendering

- Replace nested conditionals with early returns or components
- Use ternary operators for simple conditions
- Extract complex conditional logic into separate functions or components

```tsx
// ❌ Bad: Nested conditionals
function UserStatus({ user }) {
  return (
    <div>
      {user ? (
        <div>
          {user.isActive ? (
            <div>
              {user.isPremium ? (
                <PremiumBadge />
              ) : (
                <RegularBadge />
              )}
            </div>
          ) : (
            <InactiveBadge />
          )}
        </div>
      ) : (
        <Loading />
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

// ✅ Good: Simplified conditionals
function UserStatus({ user }) {
  if (!user) return <Loading />;
  if (!user.isActive) return <InactiveBadge />;
  
  return user.isPremium ? <PremiumBadge /> : <RegularBadge />;
}
```

## Maintaining Type Safety

- Keep props interfaces/types in sync with component usage
- Use TypeScript utilities (Pick, Omit, Partial) to derive types from existing ones
- Add explicit return types for functions and components
- Validate refactored component props with a thorough test

```tsx
// Before refactoring
interface UserProfileProps {
  user: User;
  permissions: string[];
  onUpdate: (user: User) => void;
  isEditable: boolean;
}

// After splitting into components
interface UserHeaderProps {
  user: Pick<User, 'name' | 'avatar' | 'title'>;
}

interface UserDetailsProps {
  user: User;
  isEditable: boolean;
  onUpdate: (user: User) => void;
}

interface PermissionsProps {
  permissions: string[];
}
```

## Performance Optimizations

### Memoization

- Use React.memo to prevent unnecessary re-renders of expensive components
- Apply useMemo for expensive calculations
- Use useCallback for event handlers passed to child components

```tsx
// ❌ Bad: Recreating functions on every render
function ParentComponent({ items }) {
  const handleItemClick = (id) => {
    // Handle click logic
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {items.map(item => (
        <Item 
          key={item.id} 
          data={item} 
          onClick={handleItemClick} 
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

// ✅ Good: Memoized callback
function ParentComponent({ items }) {
  const handleItemClick = useCallback((id) => {
    // Handle click logic
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      {items.map(item => (
        <Item 
          key={item.id} 
          data={item} 
          onClick={handleItemClick} 
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Virtual Lists

- Replace large lists with virtualized alternatives (react-window, react-virtualized)
- Consider breaking large tables or lists into paginated views

## State Management Refactoring

### Local vs. Global State

- Move shared state up to common ancestors or into a global state manager
- Keep component-specific state local with useState or useReducer
- Consider context for state shared across many components

### State Colocation

- Keep state as close as possible to where it's used
- Avoid prop drilling by using context or state management libraries

## Handling Side Effects

- Move side effects out of render functions
- Cleanup side effects properly in useEffect
- Keep side effects focused and isolated

```tsx
// ❌ Bad: Unmanaged side effects
function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  
  // Missing dependency array
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => setUser(data));
    
    // Missing cleanup
    const subscription = subscribeToUserUpdates(userId);
  });
  
  // Component rendering...
}

// ✅ Good: Properly managed side effects
function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    let isMounted = true;
    
    async function fetchUser() {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
        const data = await response.json();
        if (isMounted) setUser(data);
      } catch (error) {
        // Error handling
      }
    }
    
    fetchUser();
    
    const subscription = subscribeToUserUpdates(userId);
    return () => {
      isMounted = false;
      subscription.unsubscribe();
    };
  }, [userId]);
  
  // Component rendering...
}
```

## Testing Considerations

- Update tests to match refactored component behavior
- Test components in isolation with unit tests
- Add integration tests for component interactions
- Consider snapshot tests for UI regressions
- Test edge cases and error states

## Refactoring with shadcn/ui

When refactoring components to use shadcn/ui:

- Replace custom UI elements with equivalent shadcn/ui components
- Preserve existing functionality and behavior
- Adapt component props to match shadcn/ui API
- Use shadcn/ui's className approach for styling
- Leverage shadcn/ui's accessibility features

```tsx
// ❌ Before: Custom button component
function Button({ label, onClick, variant = "primary" }) {
  return (
    <button 
      className={`btn ${variant === "primary" ? "btn-primary" : "btn-secondary"}`}
      onClick={onClick}
    >
      {label}
    </button>
  );
}

// ✅ After: Using shadcn/ui Button
import { Button } from "~/components/ui/button";

function MyComponent({ label, onClick, variant = "default" }) {
  return (
    <Button 
      variant={variant} 
      onClick={onClick}
    >
      {label}
    </Button>
  );
}
```

## Step-by-Step Refactoring Process

1. **Analyze the component** - Identify issues and refactoring opportunities
2. **Write or update tests** - Ensure you have test coverage before refactoring
3. **Extract smaller components** - Break down the component into logical pieces
4. **Extract custom hooks** - Move stateful logic into reusable hooks
5. **Optimize performance** - Apply memoization and other optimizations
6. **Update types** - Ensure TypeScript types are accurate and comprehensive
7. **Run tests** - Verify the refactored code passes all tests
8. **Review and document** - Add comments for complex logic and update documentation

## Common Pitfalls to Avoid

- **Premature optimization** - Don't optimize until you've identified performance issues
- **Over-engineering** - Keep components as simple as possible
- **Breaking changes** - Maintain the same API when refactoring exported components
- **Inconsistent abstractions** - Maintain consistency across your codebase
- **Circular dependencies** - Be careful about import references between components
